          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Labor Philosophy</strong><br>\nFord's approach to labor was groundbreaking yet controversial. He introduced higher wages and reduced work hours, setting a new standard for the industry. However, his company was also known for its resistance to labor unions, and Ford used strong tactics to oppose them throughout his career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Views</strong><br>\nHenry Ford had varied interests outside of automobiles, including education, history, and dance. He collected historical buildings at Greenfield Village in Michigan. Ford's views on many subjects, including war and industry, were widely publicized, sometimes causing admiration or controversy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nHenry Ford passed away on April 7, 1947. His legacy extends beyond the Ford Motor Company. He transformed the automobile from a luxury item into an essential part of American life. His innovations in production and labor practices had a profound impact on industry and society.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
